  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/style.css" />
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/features/info.css" />
  <script src="/lib/MobileNavHandler.js" defer></script>
  <%- include('./partials/icons') %>
</head>
<%- include('./partials/google') %>

<body>
  <%- include('./partials/nav') %>

  <div class="container-fluid p-3 info-container">
    <div class="intro text-center">
      <h1>Our Story</h1>
      <hr>
    </div>

    <div class="quick-links">
      <a href="#how-it-began">How It Began</a>
      <a href="#what-we-built">What We Built</a>
      <a href="#who-we-are">Who We Are</a>
      <a href="#what-we-believe">What We Believe</a>
      <a href="#aethrend">Aethrend</a>
      <a href="#our-mission">Our Mission</a>
    </div>

    <section id="how-it-began">
      <h2>How It Began</h2>
      <p>
        Far Reach Co. started the way a lot of campaigns do -
        <strong>a group of friends wanting to play together, even though we were far apart.</strong>
        Some of us were in California, some in Florida, and for a while one of us was playing from Italy.
        The only way to keep our game alive was online.
      </p>
      <p>
        We tried the popular platforms. They worked, but something was off. Too many menus. Too much setup.
        Too much time spent fighting the software instead of playing the game.
        We wanted something that let us <strong>focus on the story instead of the platform</strong>.
        Something faster. Something simpler.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="what-we-built">
      <h2>What We Built</h2>
      <p>
        So we started building our own tools. First came world-building features for organizing our campaign.
        Then <strong>custom character sheets for D&D 5E</strong> that felt more like paper than software.
        Then a <strong>Virtual Tabletop</strong> - a clean canvas uncluttered by features we didn't need.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every decision came back to the same question: <strong>does this help us play, or does it get in the way?</strong>
        If it got in the way, we cut it. What remained was a platform built around the things that actually matter -
        speed, simplicity, and staying immersed in the game.
      </p>
      <div class="toolshot-grid toolshot-grid--two">
        <figure class="toolshot-card">
          <img
            src="/assets/screen_shots/vtt-screenshot.png"
            alt="VTT map with tokens and drawing annotations."
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>Virtual tabletop for maps, tokens, and live play.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="toolshot-card">
          <img
            src="/assets/screen_shots/character_sheet_adren.png"
            alt="Character sheet editor with core stats and attacks."
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>Character sheets designed for quick, in-session edits.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="toolshot-card">
          <img
            src="/assets/screen_shots/library_screenshot.png"
            alt="Library app with image folders and organized assets."
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>Library tools for organizing and reusing session assets.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="toolshot-card toolshot-card--smallshot">
          <img
            src="/assets/screen_shots/radio_host_control.png"
            alt="Radio host controls with playlist and listener link."
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>Synced music controls for session ambience.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="who-we-are">
      <h2>Who We Are</h2>
      <p>
        We're not a big studio. We're a handful of
        <strong>musicians, artists, developers, and teachers</strong>
        who came together through storytelling. What holds us together isn't business - it's friendship,
        and the motivation to keep that friendship alive whether we're across the table or across the ocean.
      </p>
      <p>
        We built Far Reach Co. for ourselves first. Now we're sharing it with anyone who wants the same thing we did:
        <strong>a VTT that gets out of the way and lets you play.</strong>
      </p>

      <p>
        Share a laugh with us! You can find our streams and content on <a href="https://www.youtube.com/@FarReachCo">YouTube</a> or <a href="https://www.tiktok.com/@farreachco">TikTok</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="what-we-believe">
      <h2>What We Believe</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Simplicity over feature count.</strong> More buttons doesn't mean better software.</li>
        <li><strong>Speed matters.</strong> Players shouldn't wait for the platform to catch up.</li>
        <li><strong>Immersion is fragile.</strong> Every unnecessary click pulls players out of the story.</li>
        <li><strong>Your assets are yours.</strong> Use whatever images you own without restrictions.</li>
        <li><strong>Getting started should be easy.</strong> Share a link. Your players are in.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="aethrend">
      <h2>Aethrend</h2>
      <p>
        We're also working on <strong>Aethrend</strong>, our own campaign setting
        born from years of homebrew adventures and streamed sessions.
        It carries the same spirit as everything we make:
        creativity, collaboration, and the joy of playing together.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="our-mission">
      <h2>Our Mission</h2>
      <p>
        To build tools that are <strong>fast, intuitive, and invisible</strong> -
        tools that serve the story instead of demanding attention.
        We're not trying to be the biggest VTT. We're trying to be the one that
        <strong>gets out of your way and lets you play.</strong>
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <%- include('./partials/footer') %>
</body>

</html>
